	General:  starting from when you hit minetown, you're in a
brutal race to improve your character's combat ability.  If you can
improve your weapon and your armor enough, you're OK clear down to
the entrances to the Castle and the Quest.  If you fail, you 
generally die.  The monsters get far tougher, and picking up a few
extra HP by going from Clev 9 to Clev 12 doesn't help you enough
to notice.

	Check out *all* armor and weapons which might be better
than what you've got now.  (Price-IDing weapons is the only practical
way to check them out.  Shopkeepers offer 5 z$ more for every extra
+1.)  Use price-IDing to find scrolls of Enchant Weapon.  

	Exercise to get your strength to 18.  Your stats have a
chance to rise about every 900 turns.  Pushing boulders for 975 /
(18 - str) turns between stat gain checks maxes out your chance of
gaining strength.  (That's the simplified version.)

	Protecting your scrolls, potions, and whatnot:  set up a
central supply dump in minetown.  Leave valuable loot there.  Use
a bag or sack for valuable stuff you don't need fast access to;
the bag protects them from everything except being immersed in
water.

	Protection against lowered stats:  apply a unicorn horn
until the star restores.  Note that fire traps can permanently lower
your HPs by 5.  Eat fire-related corpses until you pick up fire
resistance.  Also, eating poisonous corpses lowers your strength
if you aren't poison-resistant.  Once you have a unicorn horn, go
for it:  the horn restores your strength, and you'll eventually
pick up poison resistance ("you feel healthy").

	Protection against succubi:  if your int + chr is 34 or
higher, you will never get a bad result.  At 24, you're at the
break even point (if you put your gold in a bag).  Below 24, kill
them (which may take two or three encounters; use ESP to track 
them down before they heal much).  (Personally, I just kill them.)

	Which reminds me:  you must have a ranged attack.  I 
strongly favor daggers.  

	Protection against rust monsters:  if you can wear non-
rusting armor without giving up AC, do so.  Your body armor should
be mithril or DSM.  When you see a rust monster, you want to take 
off all your rusting armor before it gets to you, and you want to 
swap to a non-rusting weapon (unicorn horn or elven dagger) before
fighting it.  You may have to use a wand of digging or a teleport
to dodge a rust monster you're not ready for.  If you hear a crunch-
ing sound, use ESP to check for rust monsters.  Use ESP every couple
of hundred turns in any case.  (Do you need ESP explained?]

	Protection against damaged and destroyed armor:  by the end
game, after you've maxed out your AC, you rustproof & rotproof your
armor.  In the meantime, have backup armor around.  It's OK for it 
to be in a shop.  Just know where you can get the second-best helm
you've seen if you need it.

	Protection against level drain:  some armor has a property
called "magic cancellation", AKA MC.  Make sure you're wearing at
least one piece of armor with MC 3.  See the armor spoiler for 
which have this.  Mithril body armor, elven cloaks, and dwarven 
cloaks all have MC 3.  (Slash'EM is ten times worse when it comes
to level drains.)

	Protecting pets:  polymorph them into something tougher.
Even this isn't enough, but it helps.  Use thrown food to tame
more cats and dogs as desired.

	Protection against traps:  none.  Auto-searching helps a 
bit.  Magic resistance helps with polymorph traps.

